WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:05.749 align:start position:0%
 
rockets struck near kabul's  international airport this morning as  the u.s troop withdrawal enters its  final stage witnesses said the rockets  hit the salim karwan neighborhood just  north of the airport where planes  continued to evacuate military and  civilian personnel the explosions were  followed by gunfire now it is unclear  who is responsible for the attack and if  anyone was injured it comes after the  u.s carried out two airstrikes on  suspected islamist militants over the  weekend  a bombed out vehicle after more rocket  strikes near kabul's international  airport on monday  witnesses say they heard several  explosions in the afghan capital  it's not yet clear who launched the  rockets  the blasts come as the u.s military  races to complete its evacuation from  afghanistan by a tuesday deadline  amid threats of attacks on kabul's  airport  on sunday a u.s drone blew up a vehicle  carrying suspected suicide bombers  apparently on their way to the airport  the u.s says the car contained a large  amount of explosives  eyewitnesses say the drone strike hit  two cars as well as a house killing  several people  suddenly the sound of mortars came and  it was very dangerous  when we came here the cars were on fire  and some children had died and their  hands and faces were burnt  many afghans say they know the violence  and uncertainty surrounding them are  inescapable and they're eager to see  basic services operating again  some banks have reopened but the lines  are long and clients can only withdraw  limited amounts of cash  many questions remain around daily life  the last time the taliban were in power  women were banned from work  and girls from going to school  now the group says they will be allowed  but under taliban rules rules that have  yet to be made clear so many pupils are  staying home  to come to school  but no one is here in our class  there are five people in a class  we want to study in our future will be  good if we can  but the situation of our schools is very  bad  unsure of what comes next afghans  especially in the capital kabul  remain on high alert  let's get the latest from kabul now we  can speak to journalist franz marty  who's standing by hi friends good to see  you what more can you tell us about the  rocket fire around kabul airport  the rockets uh impacted like in in areas  around the airport but apparently didn't  cause um  a lot of damage or or casualties or not  that  as far as i'm aware  they have been aimed at the airport it  seems they were fired from a launcher  hidden in a car  this modus operandi is like a hallmark  of the self-declared islamic state so  far however no one has officially  claimed the responsibility it should  also be taken into account that such  rockets attacks are nothing new there  have been like several during the past  two three years um so it is not  something completely new  well the evacuations are drawing to a  close are afghans still going to the  airport to try to get out  there's still some afghans that try to  get into into the airport however  usually they try to  go  with a bus there have been like buses  organized by several evacuation flights  that then pick people up outside and are  supposed to bring them inside this  worked like until yesterday or so but  from a person  who tried to help evacuees i have heard  that like the last civilian got into the  airport yesterday and also only with a  lot of problems so now it seems to be  more or less closed but there's  still some people that hope  against all odds to make it in  and france what about the taliban in all  of this what restrictions are they  imposing on people who want to leave  so far it has been like chaotic scenes  at the airport so the taliban uh  supposedly um  uh kept only people out without uh  paperwork but there have been like many  instances reported where like even even  passport holders afghans with a foreign  passport were not let through by the  taliban  um after the attack they pushed the  parameter further away and uh let him  less people in if at all um so there  have been reports that they uh  like double checked like manifests uh on  on these buses that really only people  who are on lists inside get in this is a  stark contrast to the to to before when  practically everyone just like tried how  it will be in the future remains to be  seen the taliban have time and time  again assured that people with valid  travel documents can also leave the  country after the evacuation uh  concludes  journalist franz marti reporting there  from kabul thank you  germany's foreign minister is on a tour  of countries neighboring afghanistan to  shore up support for continued  evacuations on monday he visited the  uzbek capital tashkent where he  announced that uzbekistan is willing to  open its borders to evacuees who will  then be flown to germany the german  government says there are more than ten  thousand people who still need to be  evacuated from afghanistan massa's trip  includes stops in tajikistan later today  and pakistan tomorrow  and dw correspondent nick connolly is  following the talks in tashkent for us  today hi nick  what is the situation like at the border  are a lot of afghans trying to flee into  uzbekistan right now  well potentially there are tens of  thousands of afghans who would you want  to use this route because from where  they are in the north of afghanistan  places like mazar sharif kunduz  these neighboring countries like  uzbekistan  are far closer than kabul and they'd  have far fewer taliban checkpoints to  get through for now not many have been  let through mainly people who already  had visas people  largely kind of upper middle class  people politicians people who had  well-connected mainly people from the  uzbek ethnic minority in afghanistan  there was a big moment on 14th of august  when mazan sharif fell to the taliban  and basically the whole military  leadership of that town tried to get  over to uzbekistan but only a handful  were actually led in permanently so  there's a real demand here from people  even i've only been a few days and  already um inundated with requests from  people friends of friends trying to find  a way out of afghanistan through  uzbekistan but not getting a chance so  far well we know that germany germany  only managed to get around 5 300 people  out of afghanistan in time but there are  a lot more people on a list to be  evacuated so what is next for them  so we heard from haikon mars today that  they would be doing their best to try  and uh start charter flights and  non-military flights out of kabul that's  obviously pretty uncertain given a the  technical side of things the fact that  the radar systems in kabul have been  largely destroyed and all that's been  taken over by the u.s military who are  leaving imminently then there's  obviously the security situation how  people are even going to get to that  airport and if as i've mentioned there  in the north of the country getting  there is a pretty precarious journey  and on which the western countries that  often employ these people can do  precious little to help people actually  achieve it in safety and then that  second option opening these land borders  but the uzbek government is very very  careful and is worried on the one hand  of  straining relations with the taliban  next door that they're going to have to  work with and secondly of turning into  basically essentially a refugee camp for  europe they are very certain and very  clear that this is only going to be  about transit and they're not going to  let people stay in large numbers for the  long term  how do uzbeks feel about afghans fleeing  to their country  well there's been a lot of criticism  here on social media of the government  especially when it comes to ethnic  uzbeks from afghanistan uh ordinary  citizens here feeling the government has  been too slow and too  parsimonious in terms of handing out  visas  but equally there is also fear of  contagions at work from afghanistan  destabilization the government here is  very worried about islamism political  islam and there is that worry that if  you know this country is destabilized by  a large influx of people then the  political situation here could get more  difficult so pretty contradictory  feelings but definitely a feeling for  many ordinary respects that this country  could be doing more  dw's nick connolly reporting from  tashkent good to talk to you

